/span><span style=\"color:#000\">k </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> zr_vec</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> diag_elem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> diag_matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">diag_elem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">D</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">            </span><span style=\"color:#800\">// basis</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\">           </span><span style=\"color:#800\">// coefficients</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  <br>  </span><span style=\"color:#800\">//vector[K] x[N];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  <br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>      x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> multi_normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">zr_vec</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>      <br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">d </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">D</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>      A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">d</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  <br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#800\">//y[i] ~ multi_normal(A * x[i], Sigma);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">d </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">D</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>      </span><span style=\"color:#800\">//y[d,i] ~ normal(dot_product(row(A, d), x[i]), sigma4);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>      increment_log_prob</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">normal_log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">d</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dot_product</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> d</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">));</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "cIUjsWwhAv0J",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2015-04-22T18:20:36Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] more efficient way to write PPCA model?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Without worrying about what the model&#39;s doing, I can give\r<br>you some performance tips.  The basic idea is that you\r<br>don&#39;t want to create a diagonal covariance matrix and then\r<br>sample from a multivariate normal.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Apr 22, 2015, at 6:02 PM, Lei Li &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">lil...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I am experimenting with Tipping &amp; Bishop&#39;s PPCA model. However, two versions I tried in Stan seem slow. Is there better way to write an efficient PPCA model?\r<br>&gt; Thanks!\r<br>&gt; Lei\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; PPCA version 1:\r<br>&gt; data {\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower=1&gt; N;              // num datapoints\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower=1&gt; D;              // num dimension\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower=1&gt; K;              // num basis\r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma1;\r<br>&gt;   //real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma2;\r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma3;\r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma4;      \r<br>&gt;   real y[D,N];\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; transformed data {\r<br>&gt;   matrix[K,K] Sigma;\r<br>&gt;   matrix[D,D] Sigma2;\r<br>&gt;   vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[K] diag_elem;\r<br>&gt;   vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[K] zr_vec;\r<br>&gt;   vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[D] diag_elem2;\r<br>&gt;   vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[D] zr_vec2;\r<br>&gt;   for (k in 1:K) zr_vec[k] &lt;- 0;\r<br>&gt;   for (k in 1:K) diag_elem[k] &lt;- sigma3;\r<br>&gt;   Sigma &lt;- diag_matrix(diag_elem);\r<br>&gt;   for (d in 1:D) zr_vec2[d] &lt;- 0;\r<br>&gt;   for (d in 1:D) diag_elem2[d] &lt;- sigma1;\r<br>&gt;   Sigma2 &lt;- diag_matrix(diag_elem2);\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; parameters {\r<br>&gt;   matrix[K,D] A;            // basis\r<br>&gt;   vector[K] x[N];           // coefficients\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; model {\r<br>&gt;   \r<br>&gt;   //vector[K] x[N];\r<br>&gt;   \r<br>&gt;   for (i in 1:N)\r<br>&gt;       x[i] ~ multi_normal(zr_vec, Sigma);\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Because Sigma = diag_matrix(diag_elem) and diag_elem is just copies of sigma3,\r<br>and zr_vec is all zeroes, this is much more efficiently done as:\r<br>\r<br>  to_vector(x) ~ normal(0,sigma3);\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "  \r<br>\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   for (k in 1:K)\r<br>&gt;     A[k] ~ multi_normal(zr_vec2, Sigma2);\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Same again\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "&gt;     \r<br>&gt;   for (i in 1:N)\r<br>&gt;     //y[i] ~ multi_normal(A * x[i], Sigma);\r<br>&gt;     for (d in 1:D)\r<br>&gt;       //y[d,i] ~ normal(dot_product(row(A, d), x[i]), sigma4);\r<br>&gt;       increment_log_prob(normal_log(y[d,i], dot_product(col(A, d), x[i]), sigma4));\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "If you can reduce this to matrix algebra with A and x and then\r<br>vectorize, it&#39;ll be a lot faster.\r<br>\r<br>The second version is similar in terms of Stan coding.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "IkOkj1pVH3UJ",
   "from": "Herra Huu",
   "author": "368f61d9252655a2",
   "date": "2015-04-22T21:40:43Z",
   "subject": "Re: more efficient way to write PPCA model?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\">It&#39;s possible to analytically integrate out either:<br><br>a) x, as was done in the original article by Tipping &amp; Bishop (see section 3.1 <a href=\"http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823856000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3U71IKqVm7QJo3CWquLFTX\">http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf</a>)<br>b) A, see article: <i>Probabilistic Non-linear Principal Component Analysis with<br>Gaussian Process Latent Variable Models</i> by Neil Lawrence (link: <a href=\"http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823856000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0QhK_9f5fJ8-wI0dbjW50O\">http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf</a>)<br><br>Both will give simple mv-Gaussian likelihood with certain mean&amp;covariance matrix. By doing option b), we can easily see that PCA correspond to Gaussian process with linear covariance function (and by changing the covariance function we can get non-linear dimension reduction models etc)<br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "NU1DBk3Kml0J",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2015-04-23T02:41:36Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Re: more efficient way to write PPCA model?",
   "parts": [
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Apr 23, 2015, at 7:40 AM, Herra Huu &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">heikki.uu...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; It&#39;s possible to analytically integrate out either:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; a) x, as was done in the original article by Tipping &amp; Bishop (see section 3.1 <a href=\"http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823856000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3U71IKqVm7QJo3CWquLFTX\">http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf</a>)\r<br>&gt; b) A, see article: Probabilistic Non-linear Principal Component Analysis with\r<br>&gt; Gaussian Process Latent Variable Models by Neil Lawrence (link: <a href=\"http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823856000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0QhK_9f5fJ8-wI0dbjW50O\">http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf</a>)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "&gt; Both will give simple mv-Gaussian likelihood with certain mean&amp;covariance matrix. By doing option b), we can easily see that PCA correspond to Gaussian process with linear covariance function (and by changing the covariance functions we can get non-linear dimension reduction models etc, these are called Gaussian Process latent variable models, GP-LVM)\r<br>\r<br>Cool --- thanks for sharing this.  I&#39;d love to hear how the direct\r<br>vs. integrated-out implementation works in Stan.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "SXo5Xclpl_kJ",
   "from": "Lei Li",
   "author": "f439e1c41db614f7",
   "date": "2015-04-24T06:26:58Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Re: more efficient way to write PPCA model?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Thanks for the pointer! <br>Yes. The integrated version is EM approach. Stan&#39;s algorithm is HMC.<br></div><div>Of course, one can even do PCA via SVD. That certainly has value. But I am particularly interested in how to make it efficient in current Stan&#39;s framework.<br><br></div>Lei<br><div><br><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Wed, Apr 22, 2015 at 7:40 PM, Bob Carpenter <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ca...@alias-i.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><span><br>\r\n&gt; On Apr 23, 2015, at 7:40 AM, Herra Huu &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">heikki.uu...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br>\r\n&gt;<br>\r\n&gt; It&#39;s possible to analytically integrate out either:<br>\r\n&gt;<br>\r\n&gt; a) x, as was done in the original article by Tipping &amp; Bishop (see section 3.1 <a href=\"http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823857000&amp;usg=AOvVaw03mExVY3QgCAtAK8bi-Vq9\">http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf</a>)<br>\r\n&gt; b) A, see article: Probabilistic Non-linear Principal Component Analysis with<br>\r\n&gt; Gaussian Process Latent Variable Models by Neil Lawrence (link: <a href=\"http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823858000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2ABry0p3q2QGF8d_DGxYl4\">http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf</a>)<br>\r\n&gt;<br>\r\n&gt; Both will give simple mv-Gaussian likelihood with certain mean&amp;covariance matrix. By doing option b), we can easily see that PCA correspond to Gaussian process with linear covariance function (and by changing the covariance functions we can get non-linear dimension reduction models etc, these are called Gaussian Process latent variable models, GP-LVM)<br>\r\n<br>\r\n</span>Cool --- thanks for sharing this.  I&#39;d love to hear how the direct<br>\r\nvs. integrated-out implementation works in Stan.<br>\r\n<span><font color=\"#888888\"><br>\r\n- Bob<br>\r\n</font></span></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div><br>\r\n--<br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to a topic in the Google Groups &quot;Stan users mailing list&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this topic, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/DYofzSAz1sk/unsubscribe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/DYofzSAz1sk/unsubscribe&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823858000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2sFSaQ9XSTWFAsMtIQkGke\">https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/DYofzSAz1sk/unsubscribe</a>.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and all its topics, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">stan-users+...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823858000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2LNOQCRlZnZOOUecLPpZAE\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</div></div></blockquote></div><br></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "iBlTEWcgGn8J",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2015-04-26T11:39:49Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] more efficient way to write PPCA model?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "You can integrate out parameters in Stan, too.  In fact,\r<br>you have to for discrete parameters.  You just need to\r<br>write down the appropriate density from which you want to sample.\r<br>You can then use the expectations of the parameters, which is\r<br>typically all you need for inference.  \r<br>\r<br>The bonus is that integrating out some of the parameters\r<br>almost always makes things faster (computing expectations\r<br>is almost always more efficient both computationally and \r<br>statistically than sampling --- it&#39;s the Rao-Blackwell theorem\r<br>in action).\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Apr 24, 2015, at 4:26 PM, Lei Li &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">lil...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Thanks for the pointer! \r<br>&gt; Yes. The integrated version is EM approach. Stan&#39;s algorithm is HMC.\r<br>&gt; Of course, one can even do PCA via SVD. That certainly has value. But I am particularly interested in how to make it efficient in current Stan&#39;s framework.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Lei\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; On Wed, Apr 22, 2015 at 7:40 PM, Bob Carpenter &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ca...@alias-i.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; &gt; On Apr 23, 2015, at 7:40 AM, Herra Huu &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">heikki.uu...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; &gt;\r<br>&gt; &gt; It&#39;s possible to analytically integrate out either:\r<br>&gt; &gt;\r<br>&gt; &gt; a) x, as was done in the original article by Tipping &amp; Bishop (see section 3.1 <a href=\"http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823859000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1pZ8rlw8vc_VQFl65koQDA\">http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf</a>)\r<br>&gt; &gt; b) A, see article: Probabilistic Non-linear Principal Component Analysis with\r<br>&gt; &gt; Gaussian Process Latent Variable Models by Neil Lawrence (link: <a href=\"http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823859000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1Mp7x7l7HVvWVjlcMm9JD_\">http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf</a>)\r<br>&gt; &gt;\r<br>&gt; &gt; Both will give simple mv-Gaussian likelihood with certain mean&amp;covariance matrix. By doing option b), we can easily see that PCA correspond to Gaussian process with linear covariance function (and by changing the covariance functions we can get non-linear dimension reduction models etc, these are called Gaussian Process latent variable models, GP-LVM)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Cool --- thanks for sharing this.  I&#39;d love to hear how the direct\r<br>&gt; vs. integrated-out implementation works in Stan.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; - Bob\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; --\r<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to a topic in the Google Groups &quot;Stan users mailing list&quot; group.\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe from this topic, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/DYofzSAz1sk/unsubscribe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/DYofzSAz1sk/unsubscribe&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823859000&amp;usg=AOvVaw16CkmUEr2BjoPLgsoRA-NM\">https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/DYofzSAz1sk/unsubscribe</a>.\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and all its topics, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">stan-users+...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464823859000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3trkQnN8cVjvsHJvF65cDO\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; -- \r<br>"
